= Transclusion =

'''Transclusion is the inclusion of content from one wiki page within another without maintaining a separate copied body.''' It makes a source reusable across articles, templates and views. Its main value is consistency: a correction to the source can reach the pages that include it.

Transclusion is '''not identical to knowledge organization, source verification, or semantic identity'''. It is a composition mechanism. A transcluded paragraph can still be misleading when used without context, and a transcluded semantic annotation may affect the page that includes it.

See [[BITwiki:Book Matter]] for identifying what content is worth reusing and [[BITwiki:Page format]] for maintaining a coherent article around that content.

== Source, inclusion and displayed context ==

Three responsibilities should remain distinguishable:

{| class="wikitable"
! Element !! Responsibility
|-
| '''Source page''' || Maintains the authoritative wording, attribution, and any qualifications intrinsic to the reused material.
|-
| '''Host page''' || Introduces the included material, explains why it belongs, and preserves the interpretation appropriate to the subject.
|-
| '''Transclusion mechanism''' || Expands the referenced source into the host's rendered page according to MediaWiki and installed extension behavior.
|}

<pre>
source maintained in one place
         ↓ inclusion
host article + context + neighboring evidence
         ↓
reader-facing presentation
</pre>

Transclusion reduces copied prose; it does not automatically synchronize the reasoning, references, or separate reading-depth explanations in all surrounding articles.

== Native MediaWiki inclusion ==

MediaWiki's [https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Transclusion standard transclusion mechanism] supports whole-page inclusion and special tags controlling which material appears. 

=== Include a Main-namespace page ===

<pre>
{{:Example page}}
</pre>

The initial colon directs the inclusion to a Main-namespace page rather than a similarly named page in the Template namespace.

=== Include a Template ===

<pre>
{{Example template}}
</pre>

A Template should normally own a reusable interface or structured rendering behavior. A page containing a substantial, subject-specific explanation is not automatically better suited to Template space.

=== Control which content is included ===

MediaWiki provides three native inclusion controls:

* <code>&lt;noinclude&gt;...&lt;/noinclude&gt;</code> — content visible at the source but excluded from inclusion;
* <code>&lt;includeonly&gt;...&lt;/includeonly&gt;</code> — content included in a host but not normally displayed on the source page;
* <code>&lt;onlyinclude&gt;...&lt;/onlyinclude&gt;</code> — when present, limits the material emitted by the transclusion to marked regions.

The tags have specific parser semantics and are not equivalent to arbitrary named-section retrieval. Test the resulting output and annotations on both the source and the host before depending on them for a consequential article.

=== Slash titles and subpages ===

A page with a slash in its title can be referenced explicitly:

<pre>
{{:Example subject/Definition}}
</pre>

However, '''slash titles are not proof that Main-namespace subpage features are enabled'''. [https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Subpages MediaWiki normally disables true subpage behavior in Main] unless <code>$wgNamespacesWithSubpages</code> enables it. Relative links, subpage navigation and moving a page with its subpages depend on that configuration. Explicit page titles can still be used as references.

BITwiki's source corpus contains both reading-depth slash titles and a small number of other slash-titled content pages; their existence does not independently verify the target installation's namespace configuration.

== Selective section inclusion ==

A named or heading-based section is not included through universal core syntax such as <code><nowiki>{{:Page#Heading}}</nowiki></code>. If the [https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Extension:Labeled_Section_Transclusion '''Labeled Section Transclusion''' extension] is installed, it supplies operations including:

<pre>
{{#lsth:Example page|Section heading}}
</pre>

The operation depends on that extension being enabled and compatible with the deployed MediaWiki installation. Its availability is '''not established merely because BITwiki documentation mentions it'''. See [[BITwiki:Deployment prerequisites]].

When selective section inclusion is unavailable or too fragile, leave the material in its source article, use core inclusion controls where appropriate, or create an explicit reusable page if the maintenance value justifies one. Do not assume that every heading is a machine-stable identifier.

== Deciding what to extract ==

A section should normally remain inline unless an independent source provides material value:

* a second substantive article needs the same wording or evidence;
* the section has a distinct review cycle, authorship or source collection;
* stable retrieval of that portion is necessary for a real workflow;
* separating it avoids repeated copies drifting apart;
* the component remains understandable without its old surrounding paragraphs.

Before extraction, check whether contextual definitions, counterarguments, limits, and citations would otherwise be detached. A strongly context-dependent passage may be safer to keep inline or to rewrite as a self-contained reusable account.

A good fragment should have an identifiable purpose, a stable source address, meaningful boundaries and documented dependencies. '''Modularity should reduce maintenance, not multiply it.'''

== Preserving epistemic meaning ==

=== Evidence and uncertainty travel with the claim ===

A claim does not become Established because it appears in an article whose general subject is well supported. Qualification of a hypothesis, preliminary finding, disputed definition or historical interpretation must remain visible when that claim is reused.

=== Context changes interpretation ===

A definition may be valid in one field and contested in another. A quotation may be accurate yet misleading after the source's assumptions are omitted. The host article must state the context required for its readers, even when the source wording is shared.

=== Contradiction is not a rendering error ===

When included statements conflict with each other or the host's own analysis, investigate the difference and make it intelligible. Do not conceal it by selectively including one position or by automatically substituting a different epistemic label.

=== Source attribution remains traceable ===

A reference to one canonical source reduces duplication, but readers still need a way to identify where the material originated and where its evidence may be checked. Revision history supports traceability; it does not replace a scholarly source citation.

See [[BITwiki:Provenance]] and [[BITwiki:Claim validation]].

== Semantic annotations can change their subject ==

[https://www.semantic-mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Embedded_format Semantic MediaWiki documents how embedded content can carry semantic annotations]. A property assignment included from a reusable page or template can be recorded against the '''host''' where it is expanded, depending on the mechanism and annotation form. A Page relation that correctly describes the source fragment may therefore become a false statement about the entire host.

Before reusing semantic markup, decide whether each statement describes:

* the source article or reusable component;
* the host article;
* a separately identified claim or subobject;
* the relation between source and host.

When an annotation must remain attached only to the source, do not rely on ordinary inclusion to preserve its subject automatically. Test the selected syntax and Semantic MediaWiki behavior, and choose explicit annotation boundaries or a separate supported subobject model as appropriate.

'''Neither source reuse nor a graph relation is proof of the cited claim.'''

== Dependency and performance hygiene ==

A transclusion introduces a dependency: editing its source can alter every rendered host that includes it.

* Inspect important inclusion sites before changing widely reused material.
* Avoid cycles, ambiguous multi-level chains and needless layers of nested templates.
* Prefer readable source locations and descriptive stable titles over numbered “final” or revision-named fragment pages.
* Keep headings, links, semantic annotations and citations intelligible after expansion.
* Consider MediaWiki's template expansion depth, node and post-expansion size limits when composing many parts.
* Test rendering, edit links and performance rather than assuming a successful source-code check guarantees them.

MediaWiki transclusions normally follow current source content; they are not fixed to a particular historical revision by ordinary syntax. If a reproducible version is essential, cite the specific old revision or preserve an explicit reviewed snapshot through an appropriate separate mechanism.

== How BITwiki uses transclusion today ==

The source-controlled <code>Template:Knowledge object</code> and <code>Template:Reading depth</code> illustrate two distinct forms of reusable interface behavior: semantic identity presentation and reading-level navigation. They do not themselves demonstrate a generalized Book Matter fragment registry.

BITwiki's additional semantic composition goals—selective component retrieval, review-aware reuse, stable fragment identity, and bounded agent-context assembly—remain design objectives where a verified implementation and clear content contract are still needed. '''Transclusion alone is not an external API, a retrieval index, or a provenance graph.'''

A reusable page should be promoted only after actual use cases justify the dependency and the resulting subject/host semantics are tested.

